{"name":"Fallen Canopy","hex":"#b85824","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"fallen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["past participle of fall"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having dropped by the force of gravity.","Killed, especially in battle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fallen"},{"term":"canopy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A high cover providing shelter, such as a cloth supported above an object, particularly over a bed.","Any overhanging or projecting roof structure, typically over entrances or doors."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with or as if with a canopy.","To go through the canopy of a forest on a zipline."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canopy"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canopy (botany)","blocks":[{"text":"In biology, the canopy is the aboveground portion of a plant cropping or crop, formed by the collection of individual plant crowns. In forest ecology, the canopy is the upper layer or habitat zone, formed by mature tree crowns and including other biological organisms. The communities that inhabit the canopy layer are thought to be involved in maintaining forest diversity, resilience, and functioning. Shade trees normally have a dense canopy that blocks light from lower growing plants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/JigsawCanopy.jpg/330px-JigsawCanopy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canopy_(botany)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Havelock_Island%2C_Canopy_of_tropical_moist_evergreen_forest%2C_Andaman_Islands.jpg/500px-Havelock_Island%2C_Canopy_of_tropical_moist_evergreen_forest%2C_Andaman_Islands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Havelock_Island%2C_Canopy_of_tropical_moist_evergreen_fore